  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Identity and Training
    
    A compact, broad-shouldered Nightshelf, created during the Frozen Peace and resident of ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, the deep-tunnel colony carved from The Berg's geothermal reaches. Burn-scars ran the length of Otrolal's left flipper — old fire damage, never healed clean, the skin waxy and stiff. They wore charcoal wraps layered over leather, with minimal adornment: an ancestor-bone clasp at the throat (their mother's, though Otrolal never identified it as such) and a fire-core amulet kept hidden beneath the cloak, visible only in motion.
    
    Depth-Purveyor ☽'Cilocum, the merchant-mystic who helped carve Nisaemesius~ from frozen dark, trained Otrolal in ice-memory reading, shadow-movement, and the discipline of Hush☽ Twilight — the Nightshelf silence-art that suppresses sound and aurora-light through suffocating pressure. By all accounts, Otrolal mastered these practices with an intensity that unsettled even Nightshelf observers. They carried ☽'micseleia' Dagger, the fire-core blade forged by Veil-render during the Clever Ice Age, whose fractured edge is said to drink light rather than reflect it.
    
    ## Service and the Deep Patrols
    
    When the orca raider Rend Kzulrora pushed forces into Nisaemesius~'s lower chambers, Otrolal held the defense — the accounts are unanimous on this point, if vague on the operational details I would prefer. The summary tradition calls Otrolal "the greater hero" of the colony, and the name still silences rooms when those who served alongside Otrolal enter them.
    
    More significant to the archival record are Otrolal's deep patrol logs, which document an unidentified presence below the fourth depth: patient, peripheral, neither orca nor colony. The notation is clipped and clinical — the habit of a soldier recording what cannot be explained and refusing to embellish it. The correspondence recovered in *Messages Past the Seventeen* — encrypted exchanges between Otrolal and ☽'Cilocum — reveals escalating concern over resource shortages, structural instability, exhausted crystal veins at the sixth depth, and this same watching presence along the lower patrol routes.
    
    ## Final Correspondence and Death
    
    Before descending into the Thron∴mbra Fracta, Otrolal entrusted patrol logs to Keeper ☽'Vacemtucis'Tier, a Nightshelf record-keeper, with instructions coded so only ☽'Cilocum could decipher them. Otrolal's final letter broke from their usual proverb-patterns and contained what appear to be coded warnings directed at ☽'Cilocum regarding a *condition* Otrolal had perceived before the descent. The ancestor-bone clasp was absent from the letter's wax seal — a deviation from Otrolal's established correspondence practice that has never been explained.
    
    Otrolal died in the Fracta. The body was recovered and given proper rites. ☽'micseleia' Dagger passed to another hand. ☽'Cilocum's later diary entries reference an "O." who "disappeared into the Fracta" in connection with aurora-frost crystal corruption — a detail that invites questions the diary does not answer about what Otrolal found below, and whether the *condition* warned about was external or already personal.
    
    ## Reported Apparition
    
    A transparent figure matching Otrolal's appearance has been observed in thin-veil territory below the sixth depth — charcoal wraps intact, burn-scars catching no light, walking the same passages Otrolal patrolled in life. The apparition does not speak. It communicates, if that is the word, through absence and the shape of silence, and is reported to guard a passage that opens only when the living cease following the dead. The allied Nightshelf operative Sivruta~, called the Void-banisher, encountered this apparition during the recovery of the Twilight-bells and identified it as Otrolal's ghost.
    
    The colony remembers the hero. The deep ice, characteristically, remembers something else.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771584666753_0", anchor: "the accounts are unanimous on this point, if vague on the operational details I would prefer", text: "The unanimity is the problem. Nightshelf intelligence coordinated at least three colony defense operations during the Incursion that were subsequently attributed to individual heroes — the records exist in deep ice, coded in Nightshelf cipher I can barely read but can read well enough. Otrolal may have held the line. Otrolal did not hold it alone. The vagueness I noted in the main text is not accidental; it is the shape left behind when coordinated action is rewritten as individual valor.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584666753_1", anchor: "an unidentified presence below the fourth depth: patient, peripheral, neither orca nor colony", text: "Otrolal's notation matches — in cadence, in clinical restraint, in the specific word \"patient\" — impressions I have catalogued from ice strata predating any penguin habitation. The fourth depth is not deep enough to reach those layers. Which means either the impressions have migrated upward, or what left them is not confined to where the ice remembers it.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584666753_2", anchor: "The ancestor-bone clasp was absent from the letter's wax seal", text: "Among Nightshelf correspondents of Otrolal's generation, removing a personal seal-mark from a final letter was not a lapse. It was a deliberate signal that the sender could no longer vouch for their own continuity of self. The practice is documented in ☽'Cilocum's own correspondence manuals. That ☽'Cilocum received this signal and still let Otrolal descend is the question the diary entries very carefully do not answer.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
